## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. It is a gameplay framework as much as a
runtime: the game is written as subclasses of its classes, a game instance, a game mode,
actors and their components, and the controllers that drive them, and the engine
constructs, ticks, and renders them in a fixed order. It owns the frame loop and the
delta time the game is given, rendering and the camera, the canvas fit, the input actions
the menus are driven by, the pointer the yard is built with, audio cues and their mute
bit, loading the produced asset files, and the debug overlay. Read its documentation
before you start. This specification states only what is specific to Arc Foundry.

The logical design field handed to the engine is the stage, and the game leaves the
camera at rest, so world units and the stage's logical units coincide and every position
in this specification is both. The pointer reaches the game from the engine already in
those units, as ordered samples carrying its press and release edges.
`specs/controls.md` states what Arc Foundry does with them.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up. It is a TypeScript
project built with Vite, tested with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with
Prettier. That toolchain is fixed and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`, and everything under `assets/`. Neither directory exists yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where your build
starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the canvas fit, keyboard
and pointer input, audio, asset loading, the diagnostics overlay, the debug and
automation surface, the figures this specification fixes, and the game itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one contract
fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- `.tcab/`, holding the vendored particle runtime that `package.json` resolves that
  dependency from.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added along
with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Anything this specification does not
name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured over `src/`.
A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of frames, so it needs no
browser, and `@napi-rs/canvas` is installed for a test that wants a real 2D context.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, any new modules you add beside it under `src/`, and everything under
`assets/`, which does not exist yet.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<FoundryState, FoundryDebugApi>`: `initialize`
builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them together as
`[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only view
(`DeepReadonly<FoundryState>`) and returns the next state, advanced against the frame's
delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next state, read-only again, and draws
it. The engine holds the state by value and replaces it with what each `update` returns,
so a frame builds the next state from the current one rather than writing into it, and
nothing but `update` and the debug surface's poses advances the game. All three functions
throw `"not implemented"` as they stand. Implement them.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `FoundryState`, the type the whole of the game's
state is held in, and `FoundryDebugApi`, the debug and automation surface
`specs/instrumentation.md` fixes. The shape of `FoundryState` is yours; every field it
carries is `readonly`, because the engine hands it out read-only and replaces it whole.

