## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The type-check,
lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own logical units
on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the
top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases downward.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the browser window
  is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect ratio, the letterboxed
  centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete stage is therefore on screen at
  every window size, on load and at any pixel density, with nothing clipped and no
  scrolling camera.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale. The pointer
  position the game is given is in those same units, so a hit test against a tile center
  needs no conversion.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update.

## The three stage regions

The stage carries three regions, fixed at every screen the yard is shown on.

| Region | Extent | Constants | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Status bar | `x` `0`–`1280`, `y` `0`–`56` | `BAR_H` (`56`) | The run's at-a-glance state and the global controls. |
| Build panel | `x` `1000`–`1280`, `y` `56`–`720` | `PANEL_X` (`1000`), `PANEL_W` (`280`) | The press, the inspector, and the wave preview. |
| Yard | `x` `0`–`1000`, `y` `56`–`720` | `BOARD_X` (`0`), `BOARD_Y` (`56`), `BOARD_W` (`1000`), `BOARD_H` (`664`) | The map's tile grid, the Load, the structures, projectiles, and effects. |

The status bar and the build panel are always fully visible, and the yard is shown whole
inside its region. `specs/hud.md` states what the bar and the panel carry, and
`specs/yard.md` states the grid inside the yard region.

## Visual design

The yard is an oil-dark concrete substation lit by the flash of discharging components.
Choose the colors and the typography, decide how each component type and quality tier is
drawn, and decide how the yard, its flow toward the collector, and its effects read.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Open yard against a wall | An open tile, a wall tile, and a fixed housing tile are told apart on sight. |
| A legal placement | A held rock's footprint shows a placement it may take distinctly from one it may not. |
| The waypoint chain | Each waypoint platform carries its order number, drawn over every structure and unit so nothing obscures it, and the flow runs visibly from the entry toward the collector. |
| Component type | Each of the eight base types reads as its own type, coded distinctly from quality so the two axes never collide. |
| Quality tier | The five tiers escalate visibly in finish and in firing-effect intensity, so a yard of Scrap and a yard of Tesla-Prime read differently on sight. |
| A combination tower | A combination tower is unmistakable beside a base component. |
| A blocker | An inert blocker reads as dead: no firing head, no glow. |
| A candidate | A rolled candidate reads as still selectable, distinct from a kept component. |
| Load health | Each unit carries a health bar that depletes as it takes damage. |
| Firing | A firing component's head faces its target, and every shot is a visible traveling projectile or arc. |
| Text | Text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, type, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
